I have a STL Vector containing some Matrices such as this std::vector foovec; There are approximately 3000 entries in this vector that I want to add up and form a new Eigen::Matrix. The naive C++ code to do this would be : for(int i = 0; i <foovec.size();i++) {
      result +=  foovec[i];
  } However I want to parallelize this operation using OPENMP. I know I cant use the reduction pragma since this is a non-scalar type. If anyone can tell me a good way to parallelize this reduction without any write conflicts, that would be great.

answers.body:
You can do this //std::vector<Eigen::Matrix<double, n, m>> foovec;
#pragma omp parallel
{
    Eigen::Matrix<double, n, m> result_private; 
    #pragma omp for nowait //fill result_private in parallel
    for(int i=0; i<foovec.size(); i++) result_private += foovec[i];
    #pragma omp critical
    result += result_private; 
} For OpenMP >=4.0 you can also do this #pragma omp declare reduction (merge : Eigen::Matrix<double, n, m> : omp_out += omp_in)

#pragma omp parallel for reduction(merge: result)
for(int i=0; i<foovec.size(); i++) result += foovec[i];

For me, using reduction(merge : ...) did not work.  It had problems with uninitialized matrices (I think). The following did: #pragma omp declare reduction( \
                +: \
                Eigen::Matrix3d: \
                omp_out= omp_out + omp_in) \
                initializer(omp_priv=Eigen::Matrix3d::Zero())

Eigen::Matrix3d sum = Eigen::Matrix3d::Zero();
std::vector<Eigen::Matrix3d> elems;

#pragma omp parallel for reduction(+: sum)
for (size_t i = 0; i < elems.size(); ++i) {
    sum += elems[i];
}

