answers.body:
Mixing C++ language threading features and OpenMP is not well-defined. (See related questions ). Basically OpenMP only refers to C++98, so the interaction with OpenMP  and threadlocal is not safe/portable. It is usually assumed that it will work, because implementations do the right thing, but in this case apparently they do not. BTW: I can reproduce the same issue with Intel compiler / OpenMP runtime. The safe and portable approach is to stick to either pure C++17 or OpenMP. With OpenMP, this means to define ptr as private: static std::unique_ptr<X> ptr;
#pragma omp parallel
{
    ptr.reset();
    #pragma omp for
    for (unsigned x = 0; x < 32; ++x) { Note that the reset is necessary, otherwise the value of ptr are undefined. You cannot use firstprivate as std::unique_ptr has no copy-ctor.

